const HQ_5=[];const OQ_5=[];const NQ_5=[];
// ===== dembski =====
HQ_5.push(
{t:"dembski",fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: Dembski & Salet place their approach in the quadrant where institutions are internalised and validated in action. Statement II: They expect symbols to work through the meaning people attribute to them, and they want to explain change rather than continuity.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:0,e:"Both are true and II gives the reasons for I: internalisation is needed because symbolic efficacy lies in the meaning people attribute to symbols, and validation in action because the authors want to explain transformation. 'Both true, but II does not explain I' is tempting, but the authors explicitly say the requirements of their subject 'lead us clearly to the fourth quadrant'."},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: Dembski & Salet accept that institutional norms usually change only gradually. Statement II: For Dembski & Salet, this gradualness shows that norms are stabilised as one and the same determinant of action.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:2,e:"I is true, but the authors explicitly deny II: gradual change 'does not mean that they are nested and stabilised as one and the same determinant of action'; it only means that all stakeholders are unlikely to change their interpretation at the same time. Choosing 'Both true' mistakes gradualness for fixity, the reading of institutions the authors reject."},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"combo",q:"Which claims correctly reproduce Dembski & Salet's typology of institutional approaches (Table 1)? I. Rational choice institutionalism treats institutions as external constraints existing prior to action. II. Historical institutionalism treats institutions as internalised but existing prior to action. III. Ostrom's and Scharpf's actor-centred institutionalism treats institutions as internalised and validated in action.",o:["I and III only","II and III only","I and II only","I, II and III"],a:2,e:"In Table 1 rational choice is external and prior to action, and historical institutionalism is internalised and prior to action. Actor-centred institutionalism is validated in action but treats institutions as external to people's mindsets, which is exactly why the authors do not choose it; III describes the sociological quadrant instead."},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"case",q:"Hypothetical: a Limburg municipality owns a closed opencast quarry about which no group has strong claims or settled ideas. Planners deliberately start a yearly festival and workshops there, rather than on the historic town square, to slowly give the area a new identity. Which idea of Dembski & Salet best explains this choice of location?",o:["Performing a rite of institution that consecrates a boundary between old and new","Using an under-institutionalised niche where the norm is not yet fought over","Relying on banal reaffirmation so that the meaning sticks without conscious effort","Following the reciprocity of expectations already shared by the town's norm holders"],a:1,e:"For Dembski & Salet 'the art of planning is to find niches of underinstitutionalised space' not subject to power struggles over the norm, and they name opencast pits as an example. A rite of institution is tempting, but it concerns consecrating a boundary, not where new practices can take root with little resistance."},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"case",q:"Hypothetical: a province launches the label 'Rivierenland Metropool' at a large ceremony. Ten years later the name is on bus timetables, weather maps and local sports shirts, and residents use it without thinking about it. In Dembski & Salet's terms, what best explains why the label still has mobilising power?",o:["The opening ceremony was a rite of institution that fixed the label's meaning for good","The label's design quality was high enough to keep convincing new audiences","The province had legal authority, so the label was valid prior to any action","Everyday, mostly unconscious reaffirmation keeps it alive, as in banal nationalism"],a:3,e:"Following Billig's 'banal nationalism', the authors argue that regions must be reaffirmed through conscious and especially unconscious remembrance, or their mobilising power fades. The ceremony option is tempting, but for Dembski & Salet no rite fixes meaning once and for all: institutions must be validated again and again in practice."},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"critique",q:"Dembski & Salet hold that institutional transformation is 'deliberate change' that planners can pursue by connecting symbols to existing norms and finding niches. Which pairing of perspective and critique of this claim is correct?",o:["Evolutionary (Moroni): planners lack the dispersed knowledge to steer meaning, as most institutions are unintended","Evolutionary (Moroni): the claim neglects how crises open short windows in which agents' choices matter most","Historical (Sorensen): the claim overrates power and forgets that rules are internalised in people's own mindsets","Rational choice: the claim underrates how much symbols shape people's basic preferences and their sense of appropriateness"],a:0,e:"Moroni's evolutionary institutionalism stresses radical ignorance and that most institutions are 'the result of human action, but not the execution of any human design', so deliberate steering of meaning overrates what planners can know. The crisis-window critique belongs to historical institutionalism (Sorensen), not Moroni, and the last option states a sociological, not a rational choice, argument."},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"nuance",q:"Which paraphrase states Dembski & Salet's view on social construction and planning most precisely?",o:["Since reality is socially constructed, planners can institute new meanings whenever a symbol suits its audience well","Social construction does not let planners create any reality they wish; symbols need institutional conditioning","Since reality is socially constructed, deliberate change of meanings is impossible and change is always unintended","Since reality is socially constructed, a symbol's success depends above all on the creativity of its design"],a:1,e:"The authors write that social construction 'does not by any means imply that planners and policy makers can create whatever social reality they desire', and that symbols 'need to be conditioned'. The third option overcorrects: they still call institutional transformation 'deliberate change', so they do not deny intentional change, only voluntaristic change."},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"excerpt",q:"A planning report argues: 'Our new skyline icon failed not because its design was weak, but because nobody recognised the story it told about them. Next time we should start from what people already practise and believe.' Which reading grounds this argument, and why?",o:["Communicative planning: a symbol fails when the deliberation on its design was not inclusive enough","Moroni: a symbol fails because a designed order cannot match the knowledge in evolved rules","Sorensen: a symbol fails when no crisis has first created the permissive conditions for change","Dembski & Salet: success lies in a symbol's embedding in people's mental maps, not in its design"],a:3,e:"Dembski & Salet argue that the power of enhancement 'does not lie in the compelling design of landmarks' but in 'the institutional embedding in the mental maps of humankind', so planners should start from existing norms and practices. Communicative planning is tempting, but the authors explicitly contrast themselves with approaches that focus on the communicative expression of the symbol itself."},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"mechanism",q:"Which causal chain correctly describes how, for Dembski & Salet, a symbolic marker can institute new social meaning?",o:["Marker issued by recognised 'knowers' → its visual quality makes the audience believe it → believers sanction misrecognition","Marker issued by recognised 'knowers' → audience inculcates its meaning → meaning is then fixed and needs no reaffirmation","Marker issued by recognised 'knowers' → audience inculcates its meaning → believers sanction misrecognition and reaffirm it","Marker issued by any actor, whatever their standing → audience inculcates its meaning → believers sanction misrecognition"],a:2,e:"The symbolisers must be legitimate (step 3, Paasi's 'knowers'), the meaning must be inculcated by the audience (step 7), and believers act as sanctioners of misrecognition (step 8), which keeps reaffirming the meaning in practice. The other chains each break one link: visual quality is not the source of belief, meaning is never fixed, and the standing of the symboliser matters."},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"except",q:"Which statement about Dembski & Salet's eight-step research model (Box 1) is NOT consistent with the text?",o:["Step 7 asks how the symbol's design can be adjusted until the audience finally accepts it","Step 3 asks who the symbolisers are and whether they are authoritative (subjective context)","Step 4 asks which social structure is reproduced and whether symbols are genuine, e.g. via habitus","Step 8 asks about sanctions for misrecognition, with believers expected to act as sanctioners"],a:0,e:"Step 7 concerns internalisation: inculcation, habituation, rites of institution and making discontinuity out of continuity, not re-examining the symbol itself, the reflex of planners the authors warn against. The other three statements match Box 1; step 4 may look odd, but the objective context indeed asks whether symbols are genuine, for instance through habitus."},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"who",q:"Which course author would most clearly endorse this claim: 'The meaning of institutional norms may be neglected, latent or manifest, and openly contested between norm holders; such fluctuation is the rule rather than the exception.'",o:["Sorensen (2024)","Dembski & Salet (2010)","Moroni (2010)","Alexander (institutional design)"],a:1,e:"The claim paraphrases Dembski & Salet's Weberian view that institutions are only the 'chance' of validation and must be confirmed again and again in practice. Sorensen also stresses contestation, but over formal rules of value capture within a developmental pathway, not over the fluctuating, internalised meaning of norms."},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"nuance",q:"Which paraphrase states Dembski & Salet's assessment of historical institutionalism most precisely?",o:["It denies that critical junctures exist, so it cannot see institutional change at all","It explains change well but treats institutions as external constraints on rational, calculating actors","It explains change well, but ignores the role of history and contingency in politics","It integrates contingency, but its focus on continuity treats innovation as a particularity"],a:3,e:"The authors credit historical institutionalism with integrating historical contingency, but say its focus on path dependency leaves it weak at explaining change: even its critical junctures treat innovation as 'historical particularity'. The first option overstates: they note that some historical institutionalists do study critical junctures."}
);
NQ_5.push(
{t:"dembski",fmt:"recall",q:"Why do Dembski & Salet say the success of the Dutch Green Heart and the London Green Belt does not answer their question?",o:["They were never meant to carry a symbolic message","They aim at preservation, not at transformation","They were imposed by national law, not by planners","They succeeded only outside the regions they named"],a:1,e:"The authors accept that these concepts work, but they aim to preserve existing qualities, and a symbol that confirms existing institutions has an easier job. Their puzzle concerns symbols that must give meaning to change; nothing in the text suggests the concepts lacked a symbolic message."},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"example",q:"Dembski & Salet use Geertz's Balinese cockfight to illustrate that…",o:["rituals only matter when they are legally enforced","symbols lose meaning when an audience is too large","rituals reproduce material capital, not social status","symbols say something of something beyond themselves"],a:3,e:"In the cockfight spectators cheer not for the birds but for their own status groups: the symbol refers to social relations that are not in the symbol itself ('saying something of something'). It is not an argument about legal enforcement or audience size."},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"define",q:"In Berger & Luckmann's account used by Dembski & Salet, a code gains institutional depth through a 'reciprocity of expectations'. This means that…",o:["each norm holder expects the others to act in the typical way","each actor bargains with all the others for some mutual advantage","each new law is matched by an equal informal counter-norm","each group expects the state to sanction rule breakers"],a:0,e:"Habitualisation becomes institutional when everyone expects everyone else to act in the typical way; its main control is reciprocal recognition, with sanctions only for those who fail to recognise the norm. Bargaining for mutual advantage is the rational choice picture the authors set aside."},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"inverse",q:"Which image do Dembski & Salet take from Bourdieu to show that practice is informed by structure but not determined by it?",o:["The amulet, which protects only those who believe in its power","The meal, whose courses form a set of coded social messages","The card game, where rules set card values but skill matters","The cockfight, where spectators cheer for their own group"],a:2,e:"In the card game the cards' values (material capital) are set by the rules, which limit strategies, but players' skill still matters: habitus informs practice without determining it. The meal is Mary Douglas's example and the cockfight is Geertz's, both used for other points."},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"not",q:"Which of these is NOT given by Dembski & Salet as an example of under-institutionalised space?",o:["Forgotten sites of industrial production","Opencast pits left after extraction","Dilapidated housing estates","Protected historic city centres"],a:3,e:"The authors list forgotten industrial sites, opencast pits and dilapidated housing estates as spaces the dynamic transformation of cities yields. A protected historic centre is the opposite: a space where established norms are strongly held, so new meanings meet resistance."},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"recall",q:"Citing March & Olsen, Dembski & Salet write that 'the logic of appropriateness ranks higher than efficiency'. What do they mean?",o:["Planners should weigh fairness above cost when they draw up plans","People obey institutions from an assumed imperative, not calculation","Efficient rules spread because people come to see them as appropriate","Legitimate rules always turn out more efficient in the long run"],a:1,e:"In the internalised view people act according to institutions 'not out of strategic calculation' but out of an assumed imperative, and institutions shape basic preferences. The statement is analytical, about why people follow rules, not a normative rule for planners' trade-offs."},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"contrast",q:"Political or actor-centred institutionalism (Ostrom, Scharpf) and sociological institutionalism both see institutions as validated in action. What separates them in Dembski & Salet's typology?",o:["Actor-centred approaches treat institutions as external to people's minds","Actor-centred approaches deny that institutions can ever change in practice","Sociological approaches ignore power and focus only on formal written rules","Sociological approaches treat institutions as fixed before any action"],a:0,e:"Actor-centred institutionalism is interesting to the authors because it studies change, but it still treats institutions as external constraints that may alter strategies, not as patterns internalised in people's thinking. Neither approach is described as denying change, and the sociological one is 'in action', not prior to it."},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"apply",q:"Hypothetical: a region is marketed abroad as a 'high-tech delta', but its own residents and firms do not identify with that image. Which point of Dembski & Salet (after Paasi) does this case illustrate?",o:["A regional symbol works only once it is enacted in formal regional law","Regional identity is mainly a matter of physical infrastructure","Regional identity has an internal and an external side to believe","A regional symbol works best when addressed to outsiders only"],a:2,e:"Following Paasi, instituting a high-tech city-region must be believed 'inside and outside': identity has an internal and an external side, and here the internal side is missing. Paasi also mentions a material and institutional shape, but the case is about belief, not infrastructure."},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"recall",q:"Why, according to Dembski & Salet, do changing city-regions (Zwischenstadt, edge city, métapolis) prompt planners to look for new symbols?",o:["Their compact form already has a clear and widely shared image","National governments require a brand for each region they fund","Their fragmented, 'nonplace' landscape lacks a rooted meaning","Their residents explicitly asked planners for new regional logos"],a:2,e:"Dispersed urbanisation crosses administrative and cultural boundaries and is perceived as a 'nonplace' without deeply rooted meaning, which creates a quest for identity and for legitimacy of city-regional arrangements. These regions are precisely not compact."},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"define",q:"Following Weber, Dembski & Salet define institutions not simply as patterns of social rules but as…",o:["the chance of patterns of social rules being validated","the formal laws that a state enforces through sanctions","the organisations that carry social rules over time","the strategic equilibria of self-interested players"],a:0,e:"They define institutions as the 'chance (or possibility)' of validating patterns of social rules, so institutions must be confirmed repeatedly in practice to have meaning. Formal laws and strategic equilibria belong to the external views they set aside."},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"recall",q:"What kind of contribution does the Dembski & Salet article make?",o:["A comparative test of symbolic markers in eight European regions","A survey of residents' attachment to regional brands and logos","A single in-depth case study of Bilbao's Guggenheim","A conceptual reappraisal ending in an untested research model"],a:3,e:"The paper is conceptual: it reappraises institutional theory, links it to symbolic theory and proposes an eight-step research model, without an empirical test. Bilbao is only one of several short examples in the introduction."},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"inverse",q:"Dembski & Salet claim that 'institutions are symbolic systems'. Which illustration supports this claim?",o:["Land prices rise wherever a landmark building is planned","Voting only makes sense to people who believe in democracy","Formal rules work best when no one notices their symbols","Rituals have value only when they are performed by the state"],a:1,e:"Institutions such as religion, land property or democracy are real only to those who believe in them, so voting makes sense only if one believes in democracy. The landmark example is about markets, not about belief sustaining institutions."}
);
OQ_5.push(
{t:"dembski",fmt:"excerpt",pts:15,q:"Excerpt from Dembski & Salet (2010, p. 617): 'The point is that institutions continuously evolve and have to be confirmed repeatedly in everyday practices in order to acquire actual social meaning. [...] The meaning of institutional norms may be neglected, latent, or manifest; it may be openly contested in the case of conflicting institutions or interpreted in different ways by small or large numbers of norm holders under changing circumstances. [...] Changing predominant perceptions of reality requires a very sound notion of existing social norms and symbols that have already evolved over time. Institutional innovation does not just mean turning the latch of the door. Rather, it is deliberate change.' (a) Which theoretical perspective do the authors adopt? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each illustrated with a direct quote from the excerpt. (10)",model:"(a) Sociological institutionalism (a strand of new institutionalism): institutions are internalised in people's mindsets and validated in action, the fourth quadrant of the authors' own Table 1.\n(b) Argument 1, institutions are validated in action, not fixed prior to action: institutions 'have to be confirmed repeatedly in everyday practices in order to acquire actual social meaning'. Unlike rational choice or historical institutionalism, which treat institutions as given before action, here an institution only exists as long as it keeps being re-enacted, which is why the authors can use institutions to explain change.\nArgument 2, institutions are internalised meanings held by norm holders: the 'meaning of institutional norms may be neglected, latent, or manifest' and is 'interpreted in different ways by small or large numbers of norm holders'. The focus on meaning and interpretation, rather than on external incentives, shows the cultural-sociological lineage (Berger & Luckmann, Bourdieu, Geertz). It also explains the practical conclusion: change 'requires a very sound notion of existing social norms', so it is 'deliberate change' but not voluntaristic; symbols must connect to what is already internalised. This contrasts with rational choice institutionalism, where people follow rules out of strategic calculation; here they act from an assumed imperative.",rubric:["Names sociological institutionalism (internalised + validated in action)","Argument 1 on validation in practice, with the 'confirmed repeatedly in everyday practices' quote or equivalent","Argument 2 on internalised meaning / norm holders' interpretation, with a quote","Explains how each quote shows the perspective, e.g. contrast with external or prior-to-action views","Quotes are verbatim from the excerpt"]},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"compare",pts:10,q:"Dembski & Salet criticise historical institutionalism as better at explaining continuity than change. Compare their position with Sorensen (2024) on how institutional change happens. How would Sorensen answer their criticism?",model:"Dembski & Salet place historical institutionalism (HI) in the 'internalised, prior to action' quadrant. Its stress on path dependency makes it 'lack the capability to explain change'; even its critical junctures treat innovation as a 'historical particularity'. Their own sociological view locates change in everyday practice: institutions are validated again and again, meaning is never fixed, and new institutions arise 'through new cultural practices, which themselves become institutionalised', gradually or in institutional voids. Change is deliberate but not voluntaristic, and planners should connect symbols to existing norms.\nSorensen locates change in formal rules and political contests. Crises create permissive conditions; in a critical juncture, productive conditions (actors, legal structures, ideology, timing) shape a choice; a reactive sequence of contestation follows. His answer to the criticism is that path dependence 'is not a matter of lock-in': contestation is continuous and incremental change constant, within a developmental pathway, and incremental change can be meaningful (lot levies, Toronto's growing use of S37 and DCs). So HI does explain change, but as bounded change shaped by power and earlier choices.\nThe difference is in what changes (internalised meaning vs formal distributive rules) and where (daily practice vs junctures plus reactive sequences).",rubric:["States D&S's criticism of HI (path dependency, innovation as historical particularity)","Explains D&S's mechanism: validation in practice, new practices institutionalised, niches/voids","Explains Sorensen's sequence: permissive/productive conditions, critical juncture, reactive sequence","Uses Sorensen's 'not lock-in' / developmental pathway claim as the reply","Names the core contrast (meaning in practice vs formal rules and power)"]},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"apply",pts:10,q:"Hypothetical: a Dutch municipality wants to turn a car-oriented business park along a motorway into a mixed living-working district and launches the name 'Smart Corridor', with a landmark tower and a yearly tech event. After three years few firms or residents use the name and investors stay away. Use Dembski & Salet's concepts and research steps to analyse why the symbol may have failed and what planners should do differently.",model:"Dembski & Salet would warn against redesigning the name or tower: success does not lie in the symbol's design but in its 'institutional embedding in the mental maps' of the audience. Using Box 1: (1-2) the markers are the name, tower and event, and the encoded message is transformation to a smart mixed district. (3) Subjective context: who issued it? If only the municipality, without recognised 'knowers' such as firms already on site, its legitimacy is weak. (4) Objective context: the area's habitus is car-based, mono-functional work; a 'smart living' symbol may not be genuine to that practice. (5) Audience: residents of nearby towns, firms and investors may read it differently. (6) Over time, competing interpretations ('the business park') may persist. (7) Internalisation: there is no sign the meaning was inculcated, so it falls 'on fallow ground'. (8) There are no believers who sanction misrecognition.\nWhat to do: build on existing practices, such as firms that already mix work and events, and look for under-institutionalised niches (vacant plots, an empty hall) where new practices can take root, so that the new meaning is validated in action and reaffirmed in everyday, 'banal' use. Change is deliberate but cannot be voluntaristic.",rubric:["Rejects design quality as the explanation; uses institutional embedding/inculcation","Applies at least three of the Box 1 steps correctly to the case","Discusses legitimacy of symbolisers and genuineness relative to existing habitus/practices","Recommends building on existing practices and under-institutionalised niches","Uses 'not voluntaristic' / validation in practice correctly"]},
{t:"dembski",fmt:"position",pts:10,q:"Place Dembski & Salet (2010) on two course dimensions: structure–agency and reproduction–transformation. Justify each placement with the text.",model:"Structure–agency: a middle, structuration-type position. Through Bourdieu's habitus, structure is internalised as durable dispositions, so practice is 'informed' by social structure but not mechanically determined by it; the card game shows that the rules set card values while players' skill still matters. Agency exists, since institutional transformation is 'deliberate change' and planners can mobilise symbols, but it is bounded: 'Symbolic markers cannot be used in a voluntaristic manner', because meaning must be inculcated by the audience. Planners steer only by connecting to existing norms or by finding under-institutionalised niches.\nReproduction–transformation: the article explicitly aims at the 'transformative potential of institutions'. It rejects the view of institutions as 'rusty old fixtures' that only explain preservation: 'Institutions are not carved in stone', because they must be validated in every new practice, which opens room for change. Yet transformation builds on reproduction: change 'requires a very sound notion of existing social norms', norms usually change gradually, and many people keep the old perception. So D&S sit towards transformation, but a transformation that grows out of reaffirmed and reinterpreted existing norms, not a break with them.",rubric:["Structure–agency: middle position via habitus (informed, not determined)","Uses 'not voluntaristic' and/or 'deliberate change' to show bounded agency","Reproduction–transformation: towards transformation ('not carved in stone', transformative potential)","Notes that change builds on existing norms / is gradual","Supports each placement with text evidence or quotes"]}
);
// ===== moroni =====
HQ_5.push(
{t:"moroni",fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: Moroni concludes that, because institutions evolve, planners should refrain from intervening in them. Statement II: Moroni holds that most social institutions that have reached us are the unintentional outcome of a slow and lengthy evolution.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:3,e:"II is one of Moroni's two core ideas, but I misstates his conclusion: acknowledging evolution 'does not prevent us from intervening upon them, but simply calls for an approach that is quite different from the taxis-oriented one'. Choosing 'Both true, and II explains I' is the classic misreading of Moroni as a defender of doing nothing."},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: Moroni stresses that cultural-institutional evolution differs from biological evolution. Statement II: In institutional evolution, as in biology, the variation on which selection works is supplied by random mutation.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:2,e:"I is true, and one of the differences Moroni names is exactly that variation in institutional evolution is supplied by 'human trial and error, by intentional agents trying to deal with problems', not by chance mutation, so II is false. The other difference is that cultural evolution passes on acquired, learnt rules from many ancestors."},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"combo",q:"Following Koppl, which features does Moroni attribute to invisible-hand explanations? I. The phenomena explained appear to be designed but are not. II. A good explanation must identify the actor responsible for the pattern. III. The explanation contains an element of 'surprise'.",o:["I and II only","I and III only","II and III only","I, II and III"],a:1,e:"Invisible-hand explanations show that phenomena that look designed are 'the results of human action but not of human design', tell a process story over time, and must contain an element of surprise. II is the opposite: their aim is to 'downsize' visible-hand or conspiratorial explanations that always look for a responsible actor."},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"case",q:"Hypothetical: a Dutch province (a) sets up a new regional development company with a board, a budget and housing targets, and (b) proposes to change the national rules on expropriation and land ownership. How would Moroni classify (a) and (b)?",o:["(a) organisational design; (b) institutional intervention","(a) institutional intervention; (b) organisational design","(a) and (b) are both forms of goal-led institutional design","(a) and (b) are both second-order taxis approaches"],a:0,e:"Moroni proposes 'organizational design' for building organisations (systematic arrangements of resources for explicit goals) and 'institutional intervention' for changing basic rules of conduct such as property. Calling both 'institutional design' is precisely Alexander's usage that Moroni finds inappropriate, because there is 'a breach along the line' between the two."},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"case",q:"Hypothetical: City A computes the optimal mix of housing, offices and parks per hectare from today's demand forecasts and fixes it in a plan. City B admits it cannot know which uses will be best in twenty years and sets general rules that let owners change uses within nuisance limits, so better uses can be discovered. Which concept does Moroni use to favour City B?",o:["Robustness: rules should not be changed too often","Allocative efficiency: given resources used optimally","Adaptive efficiency: rules that support discovery","Knowability: rules must be known in advance"],a:2,e:"Moroni, following North, argues that the aim of land-use institutions cannot be 'an efficient allocation of given resources', because resources and preferences are not given; rules should support a continuous process of discovery: adaptive efficiency. City A is the allocative approach; robustness and knowability are real principles but do not capture the discovery argument in the case."},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"critique",q:"Moroni defends property and freedom of contract as evolved institutions that serve 'the welfare of everyone'. Which pairing of perspective and critique is correct?",o:["Sociological (Dembski & Salet): property rules are external constraints that rational actors simply calculate with","Rational choice (Taylor): owners do not lobby, so property rules are neutral and efficient by nature","Evolutionary (Hayek): property was designed by founders and should be redesigned to fit new goals","Historical (Sorensen): property and value capture rules are distributive outcomes of contested power struggles"],a:3,e:"Historical institutionalism frames institutions as 'unequally distributive, produced by and constitutive of unequal power relations, and contested', so selection of rules is not a benign process serving everyone. The other pairings contradict their own perspective: Dembski & Salet stress internalisation, Taylor shows owners rent-seeking, and Hayek rejects design by founders."},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"nuance",q:"Which paraphrase states Moroni's view of Alexander's three levels of institutional design (macro, meso, micro) most precisely?",o:["The three levels form a continuum, so design methods can be scaled up from organisations to society","Micro and meso design are valuable, but a qualitative breach separates them from the macro level","The whole framework is useless, since not even organisations can be deliberately designed","The macro level is fine, but micro and meso design wrongly ignore the knowledge in evolved rules"],a:1,e:"Moroni calls Alexander's micro and meso insights 'exceptionally insightful and useful', but sees 'no continuum among the three levels, but a breach along the line' between organisations and macro-level institutions. The third option overstates his critique: organisations are exactly what can be designed."},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"nuance",q:"Which paraphrase states Moroni's two core ideas of the evolutionary theory of institutions most precisely?",o:["Most institutions are unintended outcomes of slow evolution and hold knowledge no single mind has","All institutions are unintended outcomes; none ever results from deliberate human decisions","Most institutions were designed by wise founders whose original intentions have since been forgotten","Most institutions are unintended outcomes of evolution, which shows they are optimal solutions"],a:0,e:"Moroni says 'most' institutions are unintentional outcomes of a slow evolution, and that evolved institutions are a 'distillate' of knowledge inaccessible to an individual mind. 'All' overstates (formal rules can be reformed deliberately, and variation comes from intentional agents), and he stresses that evolution yields sub-optimal results."},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"mechanism",q:"Which chain correctly describes institutional evolution as Moroni presents it?",o:["Random mutations generate rules → selection reduces variety → selected rules persist through learning","People try out practices → selection reduces variety → selected rules persist through biological inheritance","People try out practices → selection steers rules to a final optimum → selected rules persist through learning","People try out practices → selection reduces variety → selected rules propagate and persist through learning"],a:3,e:"Evolution is trial and error in three steps: generation of variety by intentional agents, reduction of variety through selection, and propagation and persistence of selected rules, which are learnt, not innate. Each other chain breaks one link: variation is not random mutation, rules are not inherited biologically, and evolution has no final state or optimum."},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"except",q:"Which statement about Moroni's kosmos-oriented principles is NOT consistent with the text?",o:["Motivational realism: rules must work even when citizens and politicians are not virtuous","Non-tabula-rasa: reformed institutions are almost invariably 'successor institutions'","Openness: rules should give hard, detailed predictability of how each person will behave","Robustness: frequent changes in rules destroy the information that the rules provide"],a:2,e:"Openness asks for universal rules for an indeterminate number of future cases, guaranteeing 'pattern-predictability' and 'certainly not hard-predictability, detail-predictability'. The other three statements paraphrase motivational realism, non-tabula-rasa and robustness correctly."},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"who",q:"Which course author would most clearly endorse this claim: 'The aim of land-use rules cannot be an efficient allocation of given resources, because neither resources nor preferences are given; rules should frame a process of discovery through regulated competition.'",o:["Sorensen (2024)","Moroni (2010)","Dembski & Salet (2010)","Taylor (2016)"],a:1,e:"This is Moroni's principle of adaptive efficiency, borrowed from North. Taylor also works with market actors, but as a rent-seeking analysis of landowner lobbying, not as a normative case for discovery-oriented rules; Sorensen argues for redistribution through value capture."},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"excerpt",q:"A policy note argues: 'Rather than drafting a new national land code from scratch around this cabinet's housing targets, the ministry should find out which existing rules have worked, repair their visible defects step by step, and keep them general and stable.' Which reading grounds this argument?",o:["Moroni: a kosmos-oriented approach of modest, gradual reform of successor institutions","Alexander: institutional design of rules that serve politically defined societal goals","Sorensen: a critical juncture in which productive conditions shape a new legal pathway","Dembski & Salet: symbolic markers that institute a new meaning for national land policy"],a:0,e:"The note combines several kosmos principles: non-tabula-rasa (no code from scratch), peculiarity (rules not subordinated to specific targets), modesty and gradualism (repair recognisable defects) and robustness and openness (general, stable rules). Alexander is tempting because the note is about designing rules, but it rejects exactly his goal-driven design."}
);
NQ_5.push(
{t:"moroni",fmt:"define",q:"Moroni bases the 'taxis-oriented approach' on the engineer's method. What characterises the engineer's problem in his account?",o:["It is incomplete and relies on tacit, practical knowledge","It is complete: one aim, controlled means, data in one mind","It is negotiated among many actors with conflicting aims and values","It is solved by trial and error over several generations"],a:1,e:"Following Hayek, the engineer faces a 'complete' problem: a single aim, control over the means and a given supply of resources, so all relevant data can enter one mind. Social problems are the opposite, 'incomplete', which is why the method fails for society."},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"recall",q:"Why, according to Moroni, does an engineering method fail when applied to social problems?",o:["Engineers lack the democratic mandate that elected planners have","Social problems are simpler, so the method is too costly","Social problems are incomplete and involve practical knowledge","Society rejects any method that has been borrowed from natural science"],a:2,e:"Social situations are intrinsically 'incomplete' and involve practical knowledge (Oakeshott) that exists only in use and cannot be written as explicit formulae. The argument is epistemic, about knowledge, not about mandates or costs."},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"recall",q:"According to Moroni, which two basic roles do institutions perform?",o:["Pattern-coordination of actions and a fund of accumulated knowledge","Allocating resources and producing public goods for all citizens","Expressing collective identity and legitimising state authority","Setting end-state goals and coordinating the actors that pursue them"],a:0,e:"Institutions ensure a pattern-coordination among individual actions and are 'a fund of knowledge accrued over the centuries, and available to all'. Setting goals and coordinating actors toward them is what organisations, not institutions, do."},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"example",q:"How does Moroni use Hume's account of the rule of stable possession (property)?",o:["To show that property must be redesigned by each new generation of law-makers","To show that property was invented by early legislators and kings","To show that property is a natural phenomenon, like the weather","To show property arose gradually as a convention, like language"],a:3,e:"Hume explains property rules as conventions that arise gradually from repeated experience of the costs of breaking them, just as languages do: the result of human action but not of design. That is not a natural phenomenon in Moroni's scheme, but an artificial yet undesigned one."},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"not",q:"Which is NOT a feature of institutional evolution according to Moroni?",o:["It passes on acquired, learnt rules from countless ancestors","Its variation is supplied by intentional problem-solvers","It is open-ended and its details cannot be predicted","It produces optimal results, unlike biological evolution"],a:3,e:"Moroni stresses that every evolutionary process, biological or cultural, leads to sub-optimal results because it works on some chance variations, not all possible ones. The other three are features he names, two of them as differences from biology."},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"define",q:"In Moroni's evolutionary view, people are seen more as 'rule-following animals' than as 'rational-maximizing animals'. What does this imply?",o:["Alone people are ignorant; rules let them use more knowledge","Individuals calculate costs and benefits before following any rule","Individuals follow rules only when the sanctions for breaking them are strong","Individuals are irrational, so planners must decide for them"],a:0,e:"Against homo oeconomicus, the individual is 'intrinsically ignorant as a single actor', and institutions help people cooperate so that they can use more knowledge than any one of them has. It is not a claim that people are irrational and need planners."},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"recall",q:"Moroni says an evolutionary explanation is 'an explanation of the principle' rather than of detail. What does this mean?",o:["It shows why specific outcomes were chosen by specific actors","It explains how a kind of order arises, not each outcome","It predicts precisely which rules will be selected in years to come","It explains only physical phenomena, not social institutions"],a:1,e:"Unlike nomic explanations in physics, which explain general and particular cases, evolutionary explanations work only for the general case: they show how a type of order emerges, without predicting details. Predicting exact rules is precisely what open-ended evolution does not allow."},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"contrast",q:"Why does Moroni say the ancient Greek split between 'natural' and 'artificial' phenomena caused confusion?",o:["It treated institutions as purely natural, like rivers or winds","It counted organisations as natural and laws as artificial","It merged 'by convention' and 'by deliberate decision'","It wrongly separated human design from human convention and custom"],a:2,e:"Hayek notes that 'artificial' was expressed by two different terms, nomō (by convention) and thesei (by deliberate decision), and their merger hid a third category. Moroni therefore distinguishes natural phenomena, artificial phenomena by design, and artificial but undesigned phenomena such as most institutions."},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"apply",q:"Hypothetical: a city council wants to rewrite local property and contract rules so that they serve one goal, building 10,000 homes by 2030. Which of Moroni's principles does this violate most directly?",o:["Peculiarity: institutions have meta-ends, not specific targets","Knowability: rules must be clear and known well in advance","Motivational realism: rules must also work with non-virtuous people","Gradualism: any change in institutions must be step by step"],a:0,e:"Peculiarity says institutions are rules with 'meta-ends' such as peaceful coexistence; only organisations can 'subordinate commitments to the preferred envisaged outcomes'. Gradualism is also at stake if the rewrite is abrupt, but the case's core error is tying basic rules to one specific target."},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"apply",q:"Hypothetical: a new rule is imposed by decree, but residents see no sense in it and largely ignore it, even though everyone knows it exists. Which of Moroni's principles best explains the failure?",o:["Robustness: the rule has changed too often to be useful","Sense-making: meaning cannot be made by administrative fiat","Openness: the rule is too general to guide people in concrete cases","Modesty: the rule copies an institution that already exists"],a:1,e:"Sense-making says institutions must not only be known but also 'make sense' to the actors, since their meaning 'cannot be created by an administrative fiat' (institutions live both outside and inside people's heads). Knowability is satisfied in the case, since everyone knows the rule exists."},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"not",q:"Which of these is NOT among the contrasts in Moroni's conclusion on how to approach institutional reform?",o:["'Learn' rather than 'educate'","'Improve' rather than 'reconstruct'","'Recognize' rather than 'invent'","'Integrate' rather than 'compete'"],a:3,e:"Moroni reverses this last pair: leave 'ample space for unending competition, rather than search for definitely solving integration'. The other three are among his listed contrasts, together with preferring indirect over direct intervention."},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"contrast",q:"Alexander replies that institutional evolution also involves design, because it results from intentional decisions. How does Moroni answer?",o:["He agrees, and calls all such change a form of 'institutional design'","He argues that evolution involves no intentional decisions","He separates artificial by design from artificial but undesigned","He argues that only organisations, not rules, ever evolve over time"],a:2,e:"Moroni accepts that institutions are artificial, not natural, but distinguishes what results from human design from what results from human interaction and 'unintentional cumulative effects'; institutions are largely the latter. He does not deny intentional decisions: they supply the variation."}
);
OQ_5.push(
{t:"moroni",fmt:"short",pts:5,q:"Explain what Moroni (2010) means by a 'second-order taxis-oriented approach' and why he thinks it is present in planning's institutional turn.",model:"A taxis-oriented approach applies the engineer's method to society: it assumes a complete problem, one aim and all relevant knowledge in one mind. A first-order taxis approach uses this to coordinate urban activities through end-state land-use plans. Moroni's point is that the same attitude can 'resurface higher up': a second-order taxis approach applies it to the basic rules of society, reshaping institutions 'strictly for the purpose of specific goals' according to someone's end-state model. He finds it in planning's institutional turn when authors such as Alexander treat institutions as just another object to design ('institutional design'), which ignores that most institutions are unintended outcomes of evolution and embody dispersed knowledge.",rubric:["Defines taxis as the engineer's method (complete problem, one aim, full knowledge)","Distinguishes first-order (activities, end-state plans) from second-order (institutional rules)","Links it to the institutional turn / Alexander's institutional design","Gives the reason it fails (evolved, unintended institutions; dispersed knowledge)"]},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"compare",pts:10,q:"Moroni (2010) and Dembski & Salet (2010) both doubt that planners can simply design institutions. Compare the grounds of their scepticism and what each still allows planners to do.",model:"Both reject top-down, voluntaristic design, but for different reasons. Moroni's ground is epistemic and evolutionary: most institutions are 'the unintentional outcome of a slow and lengthy evolution', a distillate of knowledge no single mind holds, and individuals face radical ignorance. Designing them like organisations is a second-order taxis error, since 'planning strictu senso cannot be applied to institutions'. Dembski & Salet's ground is cultural and interpretive: institutions are internalised in mental maps and must be validated in action, so a symbol only works when its meaning is inculcated by the audience; 'symbolic markers cannot be used in a voluntaristic manner'.\nWhat remains for planners also differs. Moroni allows 'institutional intervention' following kosmos principles: modest, gradual, open and general rules, recognising what has evolved rather than inventing, aiming at adaptive efficiency. Dembski & Salet are more activist: institutional transformation is 'deliberate change', and the art of planning is to connect symbols to existing norms and to find under-institutionalised niches. Moroni would keep planners as careful keepers of general rules; D&S see them as mobilisers of meaning. There is also overlap: Moroni's sense-making principle (meaning cannot be created by administrative fiat) is close to D&S's argument.",rubric:["Moroni's ground: unintended evolution, dispersed knowledge, radical ignorance","D&S's ground: internalised meaning, validation in action, inculcation","Moroni's prescription: kosmos principles / institutional intervention","D&S's prescription: deliberate change via existing norms and niches","Notes a similarity (e.g. sense-making, anti-voluntarism) or a clear difference in planner role"]},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"apply",pts:10,q:"Hypothetical: the Dutch cabinet proposes to abolish all existing land-use and property rules and replace them by one new code, designed by experts, that steers every municipality towards fixed national targets for housing, energy and nature. Assess this proposal with Moroni's kosmos-oriented principles. What would he recommend instead?",model:"Moroni would see a clear second-order taxis approach: society's basic rules are 'mentally rebuilt from the ground up' around someone's end-state model. Several kosmos principles are violated. Non-tabula-rasa: one never intervenes in a blank situation; reformed institutions are 'successor institutions', and abolishing all rules discards the knowledge they embody. Peculiarity: institutions are rules with meta-ends (peaceful coexistence), so tying them to fixed targets treats them as organisations. Modesty: it 'invents' rather than 'recognises' what has worked, such as property and freedom of contract. Robustness and gradualism: a total replacement destroys the information rules provide and confuses developers and citizens; change should repair recognisable defects step by step. Adaptive efficiency: fixed targets assume we know the best allocation, whereas rules should support discovery. Openness: steering every municipality suggests case-specific detail instead of general rules.\nInstead he would recommend 'institutional intervention': keep the evolved framework, identify specific defects and correct them gradually, prefer general, stable and knowable rules and indirect intervention, and let land uses emerge through regulated competition. Targets could still guide organisations, such as public agencies, but not the basic rules.",rubric:["Identifies the proposal as second-order taxis","Applies at least four kosmos principles correctly to the case","Explains the knowledge argument (radical ignorance, evolved knowledge)","Recommends gradual, modest, general-rule institutional intervention","Distinguishes institutions from organisations where targets may legitimately apply"]},
{t:"moroni",fmt:"position",pts:10,q:"Place Moroni (2010) on the course dimensions deliberate–emergent and universal–situated knowledge. Justify each placement with the text, and note one tension in his position.",model:"Deliberate–emergent: strongly emergent. Most institutions are 'the result of human action, but not the execution of any human design' (Ferguson) and 'the unintentional outcome of a slow and lengthy evolution over time'. He uses invisible-hand explanations (Jacobs on safe streets, Schelling on segregation) to downsize explanations that look for a designer. Yet he is not purely emergent: variation comes from intentional agents, and deliberate 'institutional intervention' in formal rules remains possible, if modest and gradual.\nKnowledge: Moroni stresses situated, practical and dispersed knowledge. Social problems involve practical knowledge that 'exists only in use' (Oakeshott), individuals are radically ignorant, and evolved institutions hold knowledge 'inaccessible to an individual mind'. This argues against expert blueprints. Paradoxically, his prescription favours universal rules: openness asks for general rules for an indeterminate number of cases, precisely because knowledge is local and dispersed.\nTension: his descriptive claim is about how institutions emerge, but his normative defence of property and freedom of contract as serving 'the welfare of everyone' does not follow logically, as he admits in a note; historical institutionalists (Sorensen) would add that rules are distributive and contested.",rubric:["Deliberate–emergent: places him strongly emergent with text evidence","Notes he still allows deliberate, modest intervention","Knowledge: dispersed, practical, radical ignorance, with evidence","Explains the link between dispersed knowledge and general/universal rules","Names a credible tension (e.g. descriptive vs normative, distributive critique)"]}
);
// ===== sorensen =====
HQ_5.push(
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: Sorensen treats Toronto's post-1997 expansion of development charges and Section 37 agreements as a new critical juncture for land value capture. Statement II: After amalgamation and provincial cuts, Toronto's development charge revenue grew from zero to almost C$700 million a year by 2020.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:3,e:"II is true, but Sorensen classes this growth as a change of practice within existing formal institutions, not a critical juncture for value capture (amalgamation was one for governance, not for LVC). 'Both true' is tempting because the financial change was large, but for Sorensen a large change in practice is not the same as a juncture in the rules."},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: Sorensen argues that path dependence in urban institutions is usually not a matter of 'lock-in'. Statement II: Critical junctures set the terrain of debate, which institutions are contested and the rules for changing them, while contestation and incremental change continue within that frame.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:0,e:"Both are true and II explains I: because a juncture fixes the parameters of contestation rather than the outcome, change continues 'within a developmental pathway representing a relatively constrained range of options'. 'Both true, but II does not explain I' misses that this bounded contestation is exactly why path dependence is not lock-in."},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"combo",q:"Which of these were permissive conditions for Ontario's 1946 Planning Act, in Sorensen's analysis? I. Weak planning powers, with a post-war housing boom expected. II. The suburbs' debt crisis in the Depression and the problem of financing infrastructure. III. The new Progressive Conservative majority's choice of a more limited law than any proposal.",o:["I and III only","I and II only","II and III only","I, II and III"],a:1,e:"Weak regulation, the infrastructure-finance problem (10 of 12 suburbs needed a provincial bail-out) and the wartime shift towards intervention opened the window. The PC majority's unexpected choice is a productive condition that shaped the specific outcome, not a permissive one, so 'I, II and III' is the tempting but wrong answer."},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"case",q:"Hypothetical: a sharp housing shortage in 2030 leads to broad calls for reform of Dutch land policy. A new coalition with a strong deregulation agenda, a fast-track bill already drafted by civil servants and a well-organised developer lobby then passes a law that cuts developer contributions. In Sorensen's terms, what are the coalition's agenda, the ready bill and the lobby?",o:["Permissive conditions that opened the window","Antecedent conditions that preceded the crisis","Productive conditions that shaped the outcome","A reactive sequence following the juncture"],a:2,e:"Following Soifer, permissive conditions (here the shortage) raise the causal power of agency, while productive conditions, such as engaged actors, ideology, legal structures, available change mechanisms and timing, shape the specific outcome. Permissive conditions is tempting because all of this happens during the crisis, but the crisis only opened the window."},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"case",q:"Hypothetical: after a new planning law, municipalities use an ambiguous clause on plan approval to demand payments from developers that the law never mentions. Court cases and appeals follow, and years later the national government amends the law to confirm the practice retroactively. How would Sorensen classify this?",o:["A reactive sequence of incremental change in the pathway","A new critical juncture triggered by the many court cases","Lock-in: the law's original design fixed the final outcome","A permissive condition that opens the next critical juncture"],a:0,e:"This mirrors Toronto's lot levies: not mentioned in the 1946 Act, demanded through subdivision powers, contested in courts and at the OMB, and confirmed retroactively in 1959. Sorensen treats this as part of the reactive sequence, meaningful incremental change within a developmental pathway, not a new juncture, and certainly not lock-in."},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"critique",q:"Sorensen analyses Toronto's value capture as a contest over formal rules among organised actors after critical junctures. Which pairing of perspective and critique is correct?",o:["Evolutionary (Moroni): Sorensen underrates the power struggles behind property and planning rules","Rational choice (Taylor): Sorensen overrates how far self-interested lobbying shapes value capture","Marxist (Harvey): Sorensen overrates how far local politics is driven by the logic of capital accumulation","Sociological (Dembski & Salet): Sorensen neglects how norms are internalised and validated in practice"],a:3,e:"Dembski & Salet would say a focus on formal rules and strategic battles misses how institutions live as internalised meanings that must be validated in everyday practice. The other pairings invert their perspective: Moroni would rather fault frequent rule changes, Taylor stresses lobbying even more, and a Marxist would say Sorensen underrates capital, not overrates it."},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"nuance",q:"Which paraphrase states Sorensen's view of the 2018–2023 Ontario legislation (Bills 108, 197, 23, 97) most precisely?",o:["It is definitely a third critical juncture, as it has already cut LVC revenues for good","It may prove a third critical juncture, but he is cautious as its effects are not yet clear","It cannot be a critical juncture, because density bonuses and development charges survive","It is only a late step in the reactive sequence that followed the 1989 Development Charges Act"],a:1,e:"Sorensen is cautious about calling it a third juncture because the consequences are not yet clear, but finds it hard to imagine it will not be seen that way. The third option misuses a real point: the basic tools survive, but he uses that to show the pathway's continuity, not to rule out a juncture."},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"nuance",q:"Which paraphrase states Sorensen's equity argument for land value capture most precisely?",o:["Without LVC, house prices will always rise, so capture is the most effective tool for affordable housing","Without LVC, new residents, who are on average poorer, must carry the growth costs that existing owners avoid","Without LVC, poorer residents pay for richer newcomers' homes or for developers' and landowners' profits","Without LVC, municipalities lack revenue; the issue is fiscal capacity rather than distribution between groups"],a:2,e:"'Growth should pay for growth': buyers of new homes are on average wealthier than existing residents, so if development pays nothing, lower-income residents subsidise the newcomers' housing costs or developers' and landowners' profits, or both. The second option overstates: whether lower LVC raises prices or profits is left open for future research."},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"mechanism",q:"Which chain correctly describes the critical juncture sequence in Sorensen's method?",o:["Crisis raises the causal power of agency → actors fight and one option is chosen → its ambiguities trigger contestation","Crisis reduces the room for agency → actors fight and one option is chosen → its ambiguities trigger new contestation","Crisis raises the causal power of agency → the crisis itself fixes the option → its ambiguities trigger later contestation","Crisis raises the causal power of agency → actors fight and one option is chosen → the choice ends all contestation"],a:0,e:"Permissive conditions 'increase the causal power of agency or contingency'; in the juncture actors fight and a choice is made among alternatives; a reactive sequence of contestation follows, then relative stability. Each other chain breaks one link: crises raise rather than reduce agency, productive conditions rather than the crisis shape the option, and contestation continues after the choice."},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"except",q:"Sorensen argues that urban governance institutions are especially prone to critical junctures. Which reason is NOT consistent with his argument?",o:["Senior governments in multi-level systems can trigger change by legislation","Urban disasters sometimes prompt the creation of new lasting institutions","Urban systems face a constant stream of new challenges and crises to solve","Municipalities have strong autonomy to rewrite their own planning laws"],a:3,e:"Sorensen stresses the opposite for Canada: municipalities are 'creatures of the province' with little legal autonomy, and provinces can unilaterally alter planning law. That dependence on senior governments is precisely why junctures are often triggered from above; the other three reasons are his."},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"who",q:"Which course author would most clearly endorse this claim: 'Path dependence is rarely lock-in: contestation over institutions is continuous and incremental change constant, but it plays out within a relatively constrained range of options.'",o:["Dembski & Salet (2010)","Sorensen (2024)","Moroni (2010)","Taylor (2016)"],a:1,e:"This paraphrases Sorensen's conclusion on path dependence and the 'developmental pathway' of bounded contestation. Dembski & Salet also say institutions are 'not carved in stone', but they locate change in the validation of internalised meanings, and they criticise historical institutionalism's path dependency rather than redefining it."},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"excerpt",q:"A policy analysis of Dutch land policy argues: 'The 2008 crash opened a window for reform, but what came out of it depended on which coalitions were ready, which legal tools were at hand and which ideas dominated at that moment.' Which perspective grounds this argument, and why?",o:["Dembski & Salet: the crash created an institutional void in which new cultural practices took root","Moroni: reform must be gradual, so the crash mattered less than the slow evolution of rules","Sorensen: a crisis gives permissive conditions, but productive conditions decide the outcome","Conjunctural analysis alone: global hegemonic ideology determined the local Dutch outcome"],a:2,e:"The passage separates what opened the window (the crash, a permissive condition) from what shaped the result (actors, legal tools, ideas: productive conditions), Sorensen's historical-institutionalist reading after Soifer. Conjunctural analysis is tempting, but Sorensen's point is precisely that global forces are mediated by local institutional contests, not that they determine outcomes."}
);
NQ_5.push(
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"inverse",q:"Which of these is an instrument of land value capture as Sorensen uses the term?",o:["A rent ceiling on existing social housing units","A development charge levied on new development","A subsidy for first-time buyers of new homes","A tax credit for owners who renovate old homes"],a:1,e:"LVC uses public powers to recover part of the land value increases created by public decisions and infrastructure, for instance through development charges, density bonuses or betterment taxes. Rent ceilings and buyer subsidies are housing policies, not recovery of uplift."},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"recall",q:"How does Sorensen describe the Netherlands in his overview of value capture around the world?",o:["Municipalities captured nothing until the Omgevingswet","Municipalities taxed the land value uplift every year","Municipalities bought land cheaply and sold serviced plots","Municipalities required developers to build all infrastructure"],a:2,e:"He contrasts Japan, where most developments contribute nothing, with the Netherlands, where municipalities long captured rural-to-urban uplift by buying land cheaply and selling serviced plots. That is active land policy, not an annual tax on uplift."},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"recall",q:"Why does Sorensen stress that Canadian municipalities are 'creatures of the province'?",o:["Provinces can unilaterally change planning law and municipal powers","Municipalities can veto any provincial planning laws that affect them","Municipalities are funded only by federal rather than local taxes","Provinces must approve every single building permit issued in a city"],a:0,e:"With little legal autonomy, municipalities depend on provincial legislation, so the main battles over LVC are fought over provincial rules of the game, as the 1946, 1989 and 2019-2022 laws show. Municipalities have no veto, which is exactly the point."},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"example",q:"Sorensen cites Mahoney's description of critical junctures as 'choice points'. What makes them 'critical' in this view?",o:["Once an option is chosen, going back to earlier options gets harder","They always lead to more democratic institutions than before them","They are moments when no actor can influence the outcome at all","They arise only after a long period of complete institutional stability"],a:0,e:"Junctures are critical 'because once an option is selected, it becomes progressively more difficult to return to the initial point when multiple alternatives were still available'. This links critical junctures to path dependence and positive feedback; nothing says they are always democratising."},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"recall",q:"Why was the 1946 Planning Act's ambiguity important for later value capture, according to Sorensen?",o:["It required subdividers to pay for all services and so ended disputes","It banned any payment by developers, so value capture had to wait","It handed planning powers to Metro Toronto rather than to the suburbs","It rejected developer payments but gave discretion over approvals"],a:3,e:"The Act rejected requiring subdividers to pay for services, yet gave local governments real discretion to refuse subdivision plans, which gave suburbs bargaining power to negotiate infrastructure and lot levies. It did not ban payments, and Metro Toronto was created later as an upper tier."},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"recall",q:"What did Ontario's Development Charges Act of 1989 do, according to Sorensen?",o:["It abolished lot levies and replaced them by a provincial tax","It gave DCs a statutory basis and linked them to building permits","It capped community benefits at four percent of land value uplift","It created Section 37 density bonusing for the City of Toronto alone"],a:1,e:"The Act gave clear statutory authority for DCs, specified which hard and soft services could be included and how to calculate them, linked them to building permits instead of subdivision plans, and let school boards levy them. Section 37 dates from the 1983 revision, and the 4 percent cap from recent legislation."},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"not",q:"Which of these is NOT a productive condition in Sorensen's (after Soifer) critical juncture framework?",o:["The engaged actors and their relative power","Existing political and legal structures","The timing and sequencing of events","The housing crisis that opens the window"],a:3,e:"A crisis that increases openness to change is a permissive condition. Productive conditions shape the specific outcome: engaged actors, political and legal structures, available change mechanisms, ideology, economics and timing."},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"define",q:"What is Toronto's 'Section 37', as described by Sorensen?",o:["A fee on every new building to fund regional sewers","A cap on how much land value uplift the province may capture","Extra density allowed in return for community benefits","A rule that lets the province override local zoning plans"],a:2,e:"Section 37 (Section 36 in the 1983 revision) allowed higher density than zoning permitted in return for specified local contributions, a form of density bonusing used heavily by Toronto after 2000. A fee on all new buildings for infrastructure is a development charge."},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"define",q:"Sorensen borrows Fligstein & McAdam's 'Strategic Action Field'. What is it?",o:["A meso-level order of actors with shared understandings of the rules","A short window in which actors' choices have more effect than usual","A spatial zone in which the province sets all planning rules directly","A coalition of developers formed to lobby against any value capture"],a:0,e:"An SAF is a 'meso-level social order' in which actors interact with knowledge of each other under common understandings of the field's purpose, power relations and rules; the 1946 Act established one for Ontario planning. The short window describes a critical juncture."},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"recall",q:"Which research approach does Sorensen use for the Toronto case?",o:["A statistical model of land prices in 35 Canadian cities","An ethnography of one development project over two years","A survey of residents' views on development charges in Toronto","A historical narrative using process tracing and interviews"],a:3,e:"He presents a historical narrative using process tracing and reactive sequence analysis, based on secondary literature, primary documents such as parliamentary debates and City reports, financial data, and eleven interviews with planners, councillors and consultants. There is no statistical model."},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"recall",q:"What does Sorensen leave open as a question for future research?",o:["Whether provinces will ever change planning legislation again","Whether historical institutionalism applies to cities and regions at all","Whether lower capture cuts house prices or raises profits","Whether Vancouver's 70 percent capture rate is fully legal"],a:2,e:"He notes that Toronto's industry stayed profitable as S37 and DCs rose, but whether reductions in LVC will reduce housing prices or increase development profits and land values is 'a question for future research'. The applicability of HI to cities is his own claim, not an open question."},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"contrast",q:"How does Sorensen characterise conjunctural analysis in the tradition of Gramsci and Stuart Hall, as distinct from historical institutionalism?",o:["It traces how one rule changes through formal legal procedures","It reads how forces and contradictions 'con-join' in a moment","It models how rational actors design rules to cut their costs","It studies how norms are internalised in people's mental maps"],a:1,e:"Conjunctural analysis reads how political, economic, social and ideological forces come together or 'con-join' in one moment, as in Hall's study of the shift to neoliberal hegemony. Tracing specific rules through formal procedures is what Sorensen says historical institutionalism adds."}
);
OQ_5.push(
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"excerpt",pts:15,q:"Excerpt from Sorensen (2024, pp. 1814-1815): 'This case is also revealing about path dependence in cities and planning, a pattern more often assumed than it is explained. In most cases path dependence is not a matter of \"lock-in,\" as for many important institutions contestation is continuous and incremental change constant, but within a developmental pathway representing a relatively constrained range of options. A common pattern is for critical junctures to establish: the basic parameters and terrain of debate and contestation over the institutions created; which institutions are the subject of political contestation and which not; the overall rules of the game defining mechanisms of institutional change; and degrees of ambiguity and freedom in interpretation of the rules.' (a) Which theoretical perspective does the author adopt? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each illustrated with a direct quote from the excerpt. (10)",model:"(a) Historical institutionalism (new institutionalism), here combined with critical juncture analysis.\n(b) Argument 1, path dependence: the central concept of HI is that earlier choices constrain later options. The excerpt reworks it rather than dropping it: change happens 'within a developmental pathway representing a relatively constrained range of options'. History matters because past choices bound the present, which is why the author can explain both stability and variation between places (Toronto's 10-15 percent vs Vancouver's 70 percent capture).\nArgument 2, critical junctures as moments that set the path: 'critical junctures ... establish: the basic parameters and terrain of debate and contestation over the institutions created'. This is the HI sequence of crisis (permissive conditions), choice in the juncture (productive conditions) and reactive sequence. The reference to 'the overall rules of the game defining mechanisms of institutional change' also shows the HI view of institutions as rules that are contested between actors and unequal in their effects, rather than internalised meanings (sociological) or unintended evolutionary outcomes (evolutionary).\nThe claim that path dependence 'is not a matter of \"lock-in\"' shows the author answers the common criticism, voiced for instance by Dembski & Salet, that HI explains continuity but cannot explain change: contestation is 'continuous and incremental change constant', so stability and change are explained together.",rubric:["Names historical institutionalism (critical junctures, path dependence)","Argument 1 on path dependence / developmental pathway with a verbatim quote","Argument 2 on critical junctures setting the terrain/rules, with a verbatim quote","Explains why the quotes indicate HI rather than sociological or evolutionary institutionalism","Notes the 'not lock-in' nuance (change within bounds)"]},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"short",pts:5,q:"Explain why Sorensen (2024) proposes land value capture as a key metric of the social equity of urban development.",model:"For Sorensen, LVC is a purposeful attempt to shift the distribution of the costs and benefits of property development between public and private actors, and between generations, in the interest of social equity. In Ontario this is summed up as 'growth should pay for growth'. Buyers of new homes are on average wealthier than existing residents, so if development contributes nothing to growth-related infrastructure, lower-income residents end up subsidising the newcomers' housing costs or the profits of developers and landowners, or both. Because LVC rules are distributive 'rules of the game' of capitalism, how much a jurisdiction captures shows how fairly development is organised, and it explains why these rules are so intensely contested.",rubric:["Defines LVC as shifting costs and benefits of development between actors","Uses 'growth should pay for growth' and the subsidy argument","Explains that new buyers are wealthier, so non-capture is regressive","Links to distributive, contested rules of the game"]},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"apply",pts:10,q:"Hypothetical: in 2027 a severe housing shortage leads the Dutch cabinet to propose cutting the costs that municipalities may recover from developers (kostenverhaal), arguing that this will speed up construction. Municipalities and housing associations protest; developers support the plan. Analyse the situation with Sorensen's concepts. What determines whether this becomes a critical juncture, and what would Sorensen expect to happen afterwards?",model:"The housing shortage is a permissive condition: a crisis that raises the causal power of agency and opens the rules on cost recovery, a form of land value capture, to change. Whether it becomes a critical juncture depends on productive conditions: which actors are engaged and how powerful they are (developers vs municipalities and housing associations), the existing political and legal structures (national government's power over municipal instruments, comparable to Ontario's power over its 'creatures'), available change mechanisms (a ready bill, parliamentary majority), the dominant ideology (supply-side deregulation, as in Ontario's Bills 108 and 23) and timing. Toronto shows the same crisis-type can produce opposite outcomes: two housing crises strengthened LVC, the latest weakened it.\nIf a law passes, Sorensen expects a reactive sequence: municipalities will look for room in ambiguous provisions, contest it through courts and lobbying, and later amendments may partially reverse it (as Bill 197 walked back parts of Bill 108). Change would mostly stay within the developmental pathway set earlier: amounts and names of instruments change, while the basic structure of cost recovery remains. He would also ask the distributive question: does lower capture lower prices, or raise developer profits and land values?",rubric:["Identifies the shortage as a permissive condition","Lists relevant productive conditions applied to the case (actors, legal structures, ideology, timing)","Uses the Toronto comparison: same crisis type, different outcomes","Predicts a reactive sequence of contestation within a developmental pathway","Raises the equity/distribution question of who gains from lower capture"]},
{t:"sorensen",fmt:"compare",pts:10,q:"Both Sorensen (2024) and Taylor (2016) study conflict over capturing land value uplift (Toronto's development charges and Section 37; Melbourne's Growth Areas Infrastructure Contribution). Compare how the two authors explain the outcome of such conflicts.",model:"Taylor uses rational choice, specifically rent-seeking theory. Landowners are self-interested 'insiders' who stand to gain windfalls from Melbourne's growth boundary expansion. Through public submissions and campaigns they discredited the expected betterment and won exemptions and a shift of liability to purchasers, so policies are decided by whether they 'survive conflict with groups with an existing economic stake'. The explanation is actor-centred and short-term: concentrated interests organise more easily and capture rents at the cost of 'outsiders' (future buyers).\nSorensen uses historical institutionalism. He also sees LVC as distributive and contested by organised actors (developers' UDI and OHBA vs municipalities' AMO), but explains outcomes over decades: crises create permissive conditions, productive conditions such as ideology, legal structures and timing shape the choice at critical junctures (1946, 1989, 2019-22), and reactive sequences of contestation follow within a developmental pathway. Outcomes are contingent and path-dependent, which is why Vancouver captures about 70 percent and Toronto far less.\nSo both see value capture as a power struggle over rents, but Taylor explains it by the incentives and organising advantages of interest groups at one moment, while Sorensen adds history, timing and the institutional pathway that bounds what groups can win. Sorensen is also more explicitly normative ('growth should pay for growth').",rubric:["Taylor: rent-seeking, landowners as insiders, interest-group success","Sorensen: critical junctures, permissive/productive conditions, reactive sequence","Notes the shared view of LVC as contested and distributive","Identifies the core difference (incentives at one moment vs historical pathway and timing)","Uses at least one concrete detail from each case correctly"]}
);
